    "status": "SUCCESS",
    "response": "In short: **no, you should not make a formal issue of the panel being all men**, nor is it likely a sign of discrimination against you specifically. \n\nHere is an analysis of why, what it might actually indicate, and what likely decided the appointment:\n\n---\n\n### 1. Why Gender Is Not the Issue Here\n* **Both candidates are men:** Because both you and the successful candidate are men, gender discrimination played no role in choosing him over you.\n* **Credibility risk:** If you raise the gender composition of the panel in an official complaint or query, it is likely to be viewed as grasping for procedural technicalities rather than focusing on legitimate selection criteria.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Is an All-Male Panel a Sign of Anything?\nWhile it isn't evidence of bias against you, it can reflect certain aspects of the organization:\n* **Institutional culture / Representation:** It may indicate a lack of gender diversity at the senior executive or clinical leadership level within the hospital.\n* **Lagging HR practices:** Many modern public and healthcare institutions have policies requiring diverse interview panels. An all-male panel suggests the hospital either lacks such policies or bypassed them.\n* **Potential \"Old Boys' Network\" (Social/Professional, not strictly Gender):** While not gender discrimination against you, all-male, long-standing panels can sometimes lean toward \"safe,\" familiar choices or internal networks rather than strictly objective rubrics.\n\n---\n\n### 3. What Was More Likely the Deciding Factor?\nWhen looking at the credentials:\n* **Your profile:** PhD in clinical biochemistry + 8 years running a section (strong academic credibility, deep technical expertise, solid operational section leadership).\n* **Selected candidate:** Master’s degree + management certification + \"broad leadership experience\".\n\nIn hospital administration, the role of **Head of Laboratory** often shifts from technical/scientific oversight to executive management:\n* **Management over Academics:** Upper management often values formal management training, large-scale budget management, workforce planning, and organizational politics over a PhD. A panel may view a PhD as an indicator of a specialist or researcher, whereas they may have been looking for a generalist executive.\n* **Scope of Experience:** His \"broad leadership experience\" may have covered multiple disciplines, larger budgets, or higher-level strategic roles outside of a single section.\n* **Fit and Vision:** In a shortlist of three with two rounds of interviews, technical competence is already established. Final decisions almost always hinge on strategic vision, executive presence, and interpersonal rapport with the panel.\n\n---\n\n### 4. What to Do About the Feedback Delay\n* **Two weeks is normal in healthcare administration:** Senior clinical panels and HR departments frequently take 3–4 weeks to consolidate written feedback, especially if approvals are required.\n* **Send a professional follow-up:** Frame the request around your continued professional growth:\n  > *\"I am following up on my request for feedback regarding the Head of Laboratory appointment. With my background running the biochemistry section, I am keen to understand what specific leadership competencies or strategic areas the panel felt differentiated the successful candidate, so I can focus on them in my ongoing development.\"*\n\nFocusing purely on leadership competencies and strategic alignment will yield actionable insight without appearing adversarial.\n",
